{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-87767922d4fab768","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9017\n3\tepic_count: 2\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9017\n11\t\n12\t**Strategy:** Keyboard accessibility conformance for core Dashboard flows\n13\t\n14\t**Triage outcome:** Not below-threshold — the scope spans two architecturally\n15\tdistinct work streams (Dashboard code remediation in the odh-dashboard console,\n16\tplus substantive conformance-content authoring: VPAT rewrite and accessibility\n17\tstatement republish), which warrant separate PR review cycles. Not docs-only —\n18\tthe Dashboard component requires code changes. Full decomposition proceeds.\n19\t\n20\t## Epic List\n21\t\n22\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n23\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Keyboard accessibility remediation for core Dashboard flows | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | Update and republish accessibility conformance documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P2 |\n26\t\n27\t## Dependency DAG\n28\t\n29\t```mermaid\n30\tgraph TD\n31\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9017-E001: Keyboard accessibility remediation]\n32\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9017-E002: Conformance documentation update]\n33\t    E001 --> E002\n34\t```\n35\t\n36\t## DAG Justification\n37\t\n38\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n39\t|------|------|-----------|\n40\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | The VPAT and accessibility statement must reflect the fixed state of the four flows, so the docs-authoring epic is blocked by the Dashboard implementation epic. Per the docs-authoring priority exception, this edge does not push E002's P2 upstream — E001 remains P1 on its own HLRs. |\n41\t\n42\tNo other edges: the two epics touch different components (Dashboard vs\n43\tDocumentation), different repos, and different work-product types (code vs\n44\tcontent), so there is exactly one implementation epic and one docs epic, with\n45\tthe single docs-blocking edge.\n46\t\n47\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n48\t\n49\t| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n50\t|-----|----------|------|-------|\n51\t| HLR-1 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Keyboard operability of the four audited flows. |\n52\t| HLR-2 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | **bundled** with HLR-1: focus-indicator consistency is applied to the same controls, modals, and flow surfaces that HLR-1's operability fixes touch — remediating unreachable controls and fixing their focus indicators is one pass over the same audit defect list, so splitting would double-touch every surface. Both HLRs are P1, so no deferral signal exists anyway. |\n53\t| HLR-3 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | docs-authoring epic; priority P2 derived from strategy frontmatter `priority: Normal` per the docs-authoring priority exception. |\n54\t\n55\tFull coverage: all three HLRs map to epics; no orphaned requirements.\n56\t\n57\t## Component Mapping\n58\t\n59\tBoth strategy components map verbatim to canonical RHAI Jira components\n60\t(`Dashboard`, `Documentation` in `.context/rhai-components.txt`) — no\n61\tnon-obvious mappings. Dashboard is confirmed in-platform by\n62\t`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md` (owning\n63\tteam RHOAI Dashboard, public upstream `opendatahub-io/odh-dashboard`), so no\n64\tonboarding chain is needed. No architecture context file exists for the\n65\tDocumentation component (existing docs product, not a new component).\n66\t\n67\t## Health Warnings\n68\t\n69\t- **Epic size overage (E001), deliberately kept:** the four audited flows\n70\t  (project creation, workbench spawn, model deployment, pipeline run\n71\t  submission) with focus traps, unreachable controls, and indicator\n72\t  inconsistencies could exceed the ~2-week guideline. It is kept as one epic\n73\t  because it is one logical change — a single remediation pass over one audit\n74\t  report with one verification walkthrough; splitting per flow would fragment\n75\t  the audit traceability and duplicate the keyboard-verification pass.\n76\t  **Re-split trigger:** re-split along flow boundaries (one epic per audited\n77\t  flow, or operability vs. indicator-consistency if the audit defect list\n78\t  shows them separable) if the audit report enumerates materially more defects\n79\t  than estimated or remediation of any single flow exceeds ~1 week of effort.\n80\t- **Missing strategy sections:** the strategy contains no Impacted Teams\n81\t  table, no Dependencies table, no formal Given/When/Then acceptance criteria,\n82\t  and no Non-Functional Requirements section. Decomposition proceeded with\n83\t  available information; epic ACs were derived from the HLRs and problem\n84\t  statement.\n85\t- **Documentation team assumed:** with no Impacted Teams table, the\n86\t  docs-authoring epic is assigned to a documentation team by convention\n87\t  (\"RHOAI Documentation\"); the owning team should confirm or reassign.\n88\t- **VPAT publication process:** the strategy requires an \"updated, published\n89\t  conformance statement\" but does not specify the publication/review channel\n90\t  (e.g. whether legal or product sign-off is needed before a VPAT is\n91\t  republished). Captured as an acceptance criterion (technical review) per\n92\t  HLR-3; if a legal gate exists it belongs to the docs epic's risks, not the\n93\t  code epic.\n94\t\n95\t## Tiered Delivery\n96\t\n97\tNot applicable — no external dependencies with uncertain timing; the audit\n98\treport already enumerates the defects and both deliverables are internal.\n99\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a46ed90d748f1ae2","agent_id":"af309e1e158376ac5","session_id":"0821e53a-a893-49db-b129-b134dffec720","uuid":"fdf97f52-2d53-4c16-8af4-c710230dfd2d","timestamp":"2026-10-25T1