{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b477e0bbebc29015","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9017\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t## Review Summary\n10\t\n11\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n12\tRecommendation: accept\n13\t\n14\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exited 0 — epic body sections,\n15\tcanonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, and\n16\tepic-size triggers all check out). No conditional branch files exist, so no\n17\tbranch-file checks apply.\n18\t\n19\t## Criterion Details\n20\t\n21\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n22\tAll three HLRs are mapped and traceable: HLR-1 and HLR-2 (both P1) to\n23\tRHAISTRAT-9017-E001, HLR-3 (P2) to RHAISTRAT-9017-E002. The traceability matrix\n24\tis present in the decomposition summary. HLR-1/HLR-2 bundling in E001 is sound:\n25\tboth are the same priority (P1) and are satisfied by one remediation pass over\n26\tthe same audit defect list and the same UI surfaces — this is not priority\n27\tcollapse (no deferrable lower-priority feature is trapped behind higher-priority\n28\twork, and the two HLRs are not at different priority levels). Priority\n29\tinheritance is clean: E002 (P2, docs-authoring) blocks nothing, and the\n30\tdocs-authoring priority exception is correctly applied — E002's P2 derives from\n31\tthe strategy's `priority: Normal`, not from the P1 implementation it depends on.\n32\tTriage is absent (full decomposition), so the 2-epic shape is expected.\n33\t\n34\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n35\tSingle edge E001 → E002, justified by Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last): the\n36\tVPAT must reflect the fixed state, so the docs epic is blocked by the\n37\timplementation epic. The frontmatter `dependencies` of E002\n38\t(`[RHAISTRAT-9017-E001]`) matches the diagram edge exactly, and the diagram uses\n39\tthe dependency → dependent convention correctly. No circular dependencies, no\n40\tinvalid edges, no konflux/license/repo-onboarding epics (so Rules 7/8/10\n41\tparallelism constraints do not apply). Critical path length is 2, within the S\n42\tstrategy heuristic (1-2). Cross-check of epic content for uncaptured\n43\tdata/artifact dependencies: E002 consumes the implemented fixes (edge present);\n44\tE001 consumes only the pre-existing audit report (not an epic output) — no\n45\tmissing edges.\n46\t\n47\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n48\tThe two epics follow component/team boundaries exactly: E001 (Dashboard / RHOAI\n49\tDashboard, code remediation) and E002 (Documentation / RHOAI Documentation,\n50\tcontent authoring) — no multi-component or multi-team bundling, and no\n51\twork-product type mixing (code and content are in separate epics despite the\n52\tstrategy bundling them as deliverables). No scope duplication between siblings.\n53\tE001's potential size overage is a managed trade-off, not a violation: the\n54\tHealth Warning explicitly keeps it as one logical change (single remediation\n55\tpass over one audit report) and records a concrete re-split trigger (re-split\n56\talong flow boundaries if the defect list exceeds estimate or any single flow\n57\texceeds ~1 week of effort) — per the rubric, a triggered, justified overage\n58\tscores full marks. The decomposition also transparently flags the assumed\n59\tdocumentation team and the unspecified VPAT publication channel.\n60\t\n61\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n62\tBoth epics are correctly typed Implementation: E001 produces code fixes plus\n63\tautomated test coverage (concrete artifact); E002 is properly\n64\t`implementation_type: docs-authoring` producing published content. No\n65\tInvestigation epic exists, and none is warranted — the strategy states there\n66\tare no open questions and the audit report enumerates the exact defects, so\n67\tthere is no uncertainty that would change downstream structure. All\n68\t`gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are null, consistent with no gates.\n69\t\n70\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n71\tBoth epics are Implementation type and correctly carry `ai_signals` (not\n72\t`investigation_signals`). Signal values are consistent with the strategy\n73\tcontent and the type-appropriate rubric: E001's `open_questions: 1` matches the\n74\tstrategy's \"None\" open-questions statement; `existing_foundation: 1` matches\n75\tthe GA Dashboard console; `repo_access: 1` matches the public\n76\t`opendatahub-io/odh-dashboard` upstream. E001's `architecture_claims: 1` is\n77\tcalibrated against `.context` — verified that\n78\t`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md` exists and\n79\tcorroborates the strategy's premise (GA maturity, RHOAI Dashboard owning team,\n80\tpublic upstream, surfacing of projects/workbenches/model serving/pipelines).\n81\tE002's `architecture_claims: 0` is accurate: no architecture-context file\n82\texists for the Documentation component, so there is nothing to corroborate or\n83\tcontradict. E002's `human_process_gates: 0` is defensible and explicitly\n84\treasoned: HLR-3's technical review is a standard internal review step, not an\n85\texternal approval board, and the unproven legal-review possibility is recorded\n86\tas a risk in the summary. Rationales in both ai-signals files match the\n87\tfrontmatter values.\n88\t\n89\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n90\tBoth epics have testable Given/When/Then acceptance criteria derived from the\n91\tstrategy. E001 covers all four audited flows individually plus an\n92\taudit-defect-recheck AC and a shared focus-indicator consistency AC (HLR-2),\n93\tand includes a build/test-suite-green AC. E002 includes the rule-mandated\n94\ttechnical-review AC for docs-authoring (review by Dashboard engineering against\n95\tthe implemented fixes, findings resolved and recorded before publication), plus\n96\trepublish ACs. No AC states an externally-controlled precondition (the VPAT\n97\tpublication-channel uncertainty is correctly held in the summary's Health\n98\tWarnings rather than as an AC). No epic body references sibling epics by draft\n99\tID.\n100\t\n101\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n102\tThe full strategy scope is accounted for: keyboard operability of the four\n103\tflows (E001), focus-indicator consistency (E001), VPAT and accessibility\n104\tstatement update/republish with technical review (E002). Out-of-scope items\n105\t(screen-reader semantics beyond keyboard fixes, non-audited flows) are\n106\texplicitly carried into E001's scope and reflected as stated exceptions in\n107\tE002's VPAT content. Strategy-level context is carried forward: the\n108\tpublic-sector renewal driver appears in E002's description, and the\n109\tdecomposition's Health Warnings capture the missing strategy sections (no\n110\tImpacted Teams, Dependencies, formal ACs, or NFR section), the assumed\n111\tdocumentation team, and the unspecified VPAT publication/review channel. A\n112\tdocs-authoring epic exists as HLR-3 requires. No conditional branches are\n113\tneeded — the audit report enumerates outcomes deterministically.\n114\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a45e980e2d70341b","agent_id":"a7e8820cd033bca93","session_id":"0821e53a-a893-49db-b129-b134dffec720","uuid":"a02274f7-4c4e-4022-a113-02c2e6a14ce5","timestamp":"2026-10-25T19:02:15.327Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b477e0bbebc29015","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9017 decomposition"}
